# Git Commit Messages

Follow Conventional Commits 1.0.0 when drafting, reviewing, or creating commit messages.
Source: https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/

## Format

Use this structure:

```text
<type>[optional scope][optional !]: <description>

[optional body]

[optional footer(s)]
```

Rules:
- Prefix every commit with a type, followed by an optional scope, optional `!`, then `: `.
- Use a short, imperative description immediately after `: `.
- Add a body only when useful; start it after one blank line.
- Add footers only after a blank line following the body or description.

## Types

Use the type that best describes the intent:
- `feat`: adds a new feature.
- `fix`: fixes a bug.
- `docs`: documentation-only change.
- `style`: formatting or style-only change without behavior impact.
- `refactor`: code change that is neither a feature nor a bug fix.
- `perf`: performance improvement.
- `test`: adds or updates tests.
- `build`: build system or dependency change.
- `ci`: CI configuration or pipeline change.
- `chore`: maintenance work that does not fit another type.
- `revert`: reverts previous commits.

Prefer one purpose per commit. If a change needs multiple unrelated types, suggest splitting it into multiple commits.

## Scope

Use an optional noun scope when it clarifies the affected area:

```text
fix(parser): handle arrays with multiple spaces
feat(api): add shipment notification
```

Keep scope lowercase and concise unless the repository convention says otherwise.

## Breaking Changes

Mark breaking API or behavior changes in one of these ways:
- Add `!` before the colon, for example `feat(api)!: remove legacy endpoint`.
- Add a footer starting with uppercase `BREAKING CHANGE:`.

Breaking changes can use any type. Commits with `BREAKING CHANGE` or `!` imply a major version impact.

## Footers

Footers should follow git trailer style:

```text
Reviewed-by: Name
Refs: AC-1234
BREAKING CHANGE: configuration now uses the new schema
```

Footer tokens use hyphens instead of spaces, except `BREAKING CHANGE`, which is allowed. `BREAKING-CHANGE` is equivalent to `BREAKING CHANGE`.

## SemVer Meaning

Use these meanings when reasoning about release impact:
- `fix` maps to a patch release.
- `feat` maps to a minor release.
- Any breaking change maps to a major release.

Other types have no required SemVer impact unless they include a breaking change.

## Examples

```text
docs: correct spelling of changelog

feat(lang): add Polish language

fix: prevent racing of requests

Introduce a request id and ignore responses that do not match the latest request.

Refs: AC-1234

feat!: drop support for Node 6

BREAKING CHANGE: JavaScript features now require Node 18 or newer.
```
